                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Corona Blossom Volume 2 The Truth From Beyond continues the story of Keiji and his friends as they trie to return R-ne to space. How will they deal with Lily, Yunie, and the Underlings? What will be discovered in this volume?\n\nLike the last volume, the writers know how to mix the serious moments with the comedic ones without the tone shift feeling too sudden. As a result, the meaningful character interactions are memorable. That being said, my enjoyment towards this volume lessened after two scenes where Keiji is mistakenly accused of being a pervert. These scenes did not make me laugh, so they were a slog to get through. \n\nThe Truth From Beyond provides the reader with enough new information to keep your interest in the story from start to finish. In each chapter, every bit of information motivates you to question and think about certain things like R-ne's origins, Keiji's old friend, and if there is something else going on beyond the characters' scope. However, I felt that the plot was not moving at the pace you would expect after the events of the last volume. The comedic and SOL moments were taking too much time away from the main plot point. While Keiji does discuss with others and try to figure out how to get R-ne to space, [spoiler]he does not really figure out a solution himself. Instead, he gets an opportunity gifted to him at the very end.[/spoiler] At least the volume ends in a way that gets you excited for the next one. \n\nCorona Blossom's characters are still likeable and consistent with how they acted in the last volume. There is no standout character like in Volume 1, though. It is nice to see how much R-ne has grown into her own person. She continues to think for herself and learn new things. Also, she cannot stop being adorable. You cannot help but love the moment in Chapter 4 where she [spoiler]pleads with the fruit seller to give her something for Shino.[/spoiler] The one with the best part in this volume is Shino herself. Learning about her [spoiler]illness[/spoiler] just makes me appreciate her character a bit more. It is impressive how she can keep smiling despite a major event from her past. \n\nWhen it comes to the visuals and music, there is nothing new to say that was not already said in my review for Volume 1. The backgrounds and CGs are still nice to look at, the animated sprites are still charming, and the soundtrack is still solid. There is no noticeable issues in the lip-syncing like during one part in the last volume. This is a nitpick, but I did notice that the animation where a character shakes their head from left is right is used A LOT. \n\nOverall, I think Corona Blossom Vol.2 The Truth From Beyond is a decent visual novel. Certain scenes may have been a slog to get through, but I cannot deny that the story has a consistent charm and is a good continuation in the story. It has heartfelt character interactions, lighthearted comedy, and some mystery to get the reader excited for the finale. If you already read the last volume, then I do not see why you would not get Volume 2. See you all again once I get through the finale of the Corona Blossom trilogy.",
